<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fianna Fáil</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fianna Fáil is an Irish political party founded in 1926 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamon-de-valera">Éamon de Valera</a> and opponents of the Anglo-Irish Treaty. It entered government in 1932 and became the dominant party of independent Ireland, leading most governments for the rest of the century. It drove the 1937 constitution and pursued economic self-sufficiency.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fianna-fail.jpg" alt="Fianna Fáil" width="320" height="136" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fianna Fáil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fianna_F%C3%A1il_logo_(2024).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Fianna Fáil was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eamon-de-valera">Éamon de Valera</a>, a former leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinn-fein">Sinn Féin</a>. The year before, he had proposed a motion that elected members be allowed to take their seats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil Éireann</a> if the Oath of Allegiance were removed. It failed at the Sinn Féin Ard Fheis, and de Valera and a number of others, including most of Sinn Féin's parliamentary talent, split from the party. His new party adopted its name on 2 April 1926.</p><p>Although it also opposed the Treaty settlement, it rejected abstention and aimed to republicanise the Irish Free State from within. It entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil</a> in 1927. Its platform of economic autarky appealed to farmers, working-class people and the poor, and it largely took over the voters of the Labour Party, whose policy was almost identical.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Fianna Fáil won the 1932 general election and formed its first government on 9 March 1932. Between then and 2011 it was in power for 61 of 79 years, and its first period in office, until February 1948, was its longest at 15 years and 11 months. Its most dominant era was 1932 to 1973, when de Valera, Seán Lemass and Jack Lynch served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoiseach">Taoiseach</a>, apart from two three-year periods of the Fine Gael leader John A. Costello. Under Lemass in the 1960s the party shifted away from autarky towards free trade and foreign direct investment.</p><p>In 1970 the Arms Crisis led Lynch to dismiss the ministers Charles Haughey and Neil Blaney, who were accused of seeking to provide arms to the Provisional Irish Republican Army. In 1985 a large part of the membership left to form the Progressive Democrats under Desmond O'Malley. From 1989 Fianna Fáil governed in coalitions. Under Bertie Ahern, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taoiseach">Taoiseach</a> from 1997, it claimed credit for helping to broker the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/good-friday-agreement">Good Friday Agreement</a> in 1998. In 2011 it fell from the largest party in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dail-eireann">Dáil</a> to the third largest, losing 58 of its 78 seats. In 2020 it entered a coalition with Fine Gael and the Green Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party used a cumann system, in which the branch, or cumann, was the basic unit, grouped into district branches and a constituency branch in each constituency. Its enduring commitments are to Irish unity, to the Irish language and to Ireland's tradition of military neutrality.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fianna Fáil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its opening words share a phrase with the name of the party Éamon de Valera founded in 1926, <b>Fianna Fáil</b>, a name the Volunteers had used earlier; ministers and a 2018 Seanad report held that the anthem carries no party meaning, since the translation came first.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amhran-na-bhfiann">Amhrán na bhFiann</a></li><li><blockquote>Eamon de Valera's <b>Fianna Fail</b> took power in 1932 and dismantled Treaty links, removing the oath and fighting an economic war with Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/irish-free-state">Irish Free State</a></li></ul></section>
